.TH RECIPE 5 "September 2026" "Saphira Linux" "File Formats"
.SH NAME
recipe \- Saphira native package recipe file format (canonical contract)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.RS
.I /recipes/<package>/recipe.sh
.br
.I /recipes/<package>/files/
.RE
.PP
This page is the authoritative specification for
.IR recipe.sh .
README and website material derive from it; where they disagree, this
page wins. The practical answer to "how does a Saphira recipe work?"
is
.BR "man recipe" .
.SH DESCRIPTION
A recipe is a POSIX shell fragment plus a payload tree. The builder
sources it (never executes it directly) to learn what to build, then
calls two functions inside disposable rootless containers:
.BR recipe_build ,
which compiles in
.IR $SRC ,
and
.BR recipe_install ,
which stages into
.IR $PKGDEST .
Splitting, signing, indexing and publication are platform machinery,
not recipe code.
.SS Source precedence
When creating or porting a recipe, use the first basis that exists:
.IP 1. 3
An existing current
.I /recipes
recipe.
.IP 2. 3
A known-working Saphira v0 recipe in
.IR /reference-package-recipes ,
read-only, never edited or built directly.
.IP 3. 3
.I /cports
for upstream guidance (dependency hints, patches, options). It must
not override Saphira filesystem, musl, init or packaging policy.
.IP 4. 3
Upstream project sources and documentation.
.SS Variables
.TP
.I pkgname, pkgver, pkgarch, pkgdesc, license, origin, repo, url
Identity and provenance. All required.
.I repo=
is validated but inert (a historical component label; placement is
always the single flat per-arch repository), and its non-empty
presence is enforced.
.TP
.I pkgrel
Packaging revision, an integer >= 1. There are no r0 packages: the
first publication of a package is r1. Bump it when a payload change
must reach the repository (filenames are immutable); never as a bug
counter. Fixes that have not yet proven themselves batch into the
next rebuild.
.TP
.I source=, sha256=
Upstream source URL pinned with its SHA-256, verified before use.
.TP
.I vendor=, sha256=
Fallback for the same purpose when no direct upstream archive fits.
A recipe whose payload comes from its own
.I files/
tree (local/meta/controller recipes) does not invent an upstream
archive merely to satisfy this rule.
.TP
.I depends=
Runtime dependencies, accepting version operators
.RB ( = ,
.IR >= ,
etc.).
.TP
.I makedepends=
Build-time dependencies, same operators. Only these (plus the seed)
enter the clean build root.
.TP
.I depends_dev=
Alpine-style convention: extra dependencies carried by the
.I -dev
subpackage, typically the
.I -dev
packages of libraries whose headers yours include (so consumers get
transitive headers automatically).
.TP
.I subpackages=
Split outputs; only
.I -dev
(headers, cmake/pkg-config metadata, static archives, .so links),
.I -doc
(man/info/doc) and
.I -libs
(standalone runtime libraries, never pinned back to the parent) are
supported. A
.I -dev
or
.I -doc
name resolves to the base recipe.
.TP
.I disabled=yes, disabled_reason='...'
The builder skips the recipe (scan reports and skips, build refuses,
resolution fails closed if a dependency would resolve from it).
.SH FUNCTIONS
.TP
.I recipe_build()
Compile in
.IR $SRC .
Network fetches here must be pinned (see
.IR source= )
and checksummed; the build sees only its declared closure.
.TP
.I recipe_install()
Stage into
.IR $PKGDEST .
Remove what belongs to other splits (server binaries from a client
recipe, upstream unit files replaced by house units, glibc-locale
leftovers such as charset.alias). Never invent payload outside the
upstream install plus house files.
.PP
Worker variables provided to both functions:
.IR PKGDEST " (staging root), " SRC " (extracted source), " BUILDDIR ,
.IR RECIPE_DIR ,
.IR JOBS .
.SH FILES TREE
.I files/
holds everything the recipe stages that upstream does not install:
service units (both systemd and OpenRC where the package is a
service), configuration, tmpfiles entries, and
.IR accounts.d/ fragments.
.SS Account declarations
A service package owns its Unix identities by shipping
.IR usr/share/saphira/accounts.d/<name> ,
one stanza per line:
.PP
.RS
user <name> <uid> <primary-group> <home> <shell>
.br
group <name> <gid>
.br
dir <path> <mode> <owner> <group>
.RE
.PP
Packaged IDs fall in 0..887 (0..199 preferred; 200..887 are documented
expansion exceptions; 888..999 are local services, 1000+ humans).
The user
.I root
and UID 0 are never package-owned; the groups
.IR root ,
.IR wheel ,
.I spokes
and GIDs 0, 1, 2 are never package-owned, and no service user may
take them as primary. A user primary group must be base-seeded or
declared in the same fragment. One package owns each identity; IDs
are never recycled, even after the declaring package stops shipping
its fragment. The builder generates install/upgrade/deinstall callers
from the fragment; the reconciler (see
.BR ensure-identity (8))
creates, converges, or (on removal) sanitizes the identities.
.SH BUILD ROOTS AND /build
Each build resolves its dependency closure repository-first into one
disposable merged root (one visible workspace per top-level build:
.IR /build/PACKAGE.buildpkg ).
The seed (C library, headers, toolchain runtimes as named packages)
is the sole foundation; no host file ever crosses into a build root.
No sudo or root anywhere in the build path.
.PP
Failed workspaces are retained diagnostic evidence carrying the
marker
.IR saphira-buildpkg-failed/v1 .
Retry requires that exact marker; unmarked collisions are refused.
Never delete, bless, or overwrite an unmarked workspace (see
.BR cleanpkg (1)).
.SH FHS POLICY
.TP
.I /bin ", " /sbin ", " /lib
Essential user binaries, essential system binaries, essential
runtime libraries plus the dynamic loader. This triple must remain a
self-contained essential runtime without
.IR /usr .
.TP
.I /usr/bin ", " /usr/sbin ", " /usr/lib
General binaries, libraries, modules and development-side library
material.
.PP
The dependency rule: a binary in
.I /bin
or
.I /sbin
MUST NOT require anything under
.IR /usr/lib ;
a binary in
.I /usr/bin
or
.I /usr/sbin
MAY use both
.I /usr/lib
and
.IR /lib .
A library used by both
.I /bin/foo
and
.I /usr/bin/bar
belongs in
.IR /lib ,
and
.I bar
linking it there is correct. Development material (headers under
.IR /usr/include ,
pkg-config/CMake metadata under
.IR /usr/lib/... )
lives under
.IR /usr
even where the essential runtime
.I .so.N
lives in
.IR /lib ;
never duplicate a runtime library into
.I /usr/lib
for visual symmetry.
/lib64 and /usr/lib64 are forbidden in seeds and payloads.
.SH EXAMPLES
Minimal tool recipe:
.PP
.RS
pkgname=hello
.br
pkgver=1.0
.br
pkgrel=1
.br
recipe_build() { make -j${JOBS:-$(nproc)} CC=gcc; }
.br
recipe_install() { make DESTDIR="$PKGDEST" PREFIX=/usr install; }
.RE
.PP
Service recipe: add dual init units, configuration, and an
.I accounts.d/
fragment as above; the builder generates the lifecycle callers.
.SH OPTIONS
This page takes no options; it specifies a file format.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /recipes/<package>/recipe.sh
The specified file.
.TP
.I /recipes/<package>/files/
Payload tree (units, config, fragments, vendored sources).
.TP
.I /reference-package-recipes/
Read-only known-working v0 catalogue (port basis, never built).
.TP
.I /cports/
Read-only upstream guidance (never overrides Saphira policy).
.SH EXIT STATUS
A recipe is sourced, never executed. Validation failures surface
through the exit codes of
.BR buildpkg (1),
.BR makepkg ,
.BR resolvepkg (1)
and
.BR checkpkg (1).
.SH "DO NOT DO THIS"
.IP \(bu 2
No sudo or root anywhere in the build path.
.IP \(bu 2
No host files into build roots (names in the seed, never paths).
.IP \(bu 2
No manual copies anywhere (payload, tools, indexes, keys).
.IP \(bu 2
No r0 packages, ever.
.IP \(bu 2
No invented upstream source for local/meta recipes.
.IP \(bu 2
No new daemons, units, or fragments without owning the identity.
.IP \(bu 2
No deleting, blessing, or overwriting unmarked workspaces.
.IP \(bu 2
No glibc-locale machinery (localedef, locale-archive) on musl.
.IP \(bu 2
No duplicate runtime libraries across /lib and /usr/lib.
.IP \(bu 2
No X, no extra editors: the system stays a disciplined
system/network/server distribution.
